ll nice things are big. (Rule 5)\n\n- Dave is nice. (Fact)\n\nTherefore, Dave is big.\n\nSo, logically, the conclusion is that Dave is big.\n\nTherefore, the statement is true.\n\nSo, the answer is A) True.\n\nYes, that makes sense.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{A}\n</think>\n\nBased on the given context, we need to determine if the statement \"Dave is big\" is true, false, or unknown.\n\n1. **Facts and Rules**:\n   - Bob is big, nice, and smart.\n   - Charlie is nice.\n   - Dave is nice.\n   - Erin is big, blue, furry, quiet, round, and smart.\n   - All nice things are big (Rule 5).\n   - All furry things are blue (Rule 6).\n   - Blue and furry things are quiet (Rule 7).\n   - If something is round and nice, then it's blue (Rule 8).\n   - If Bob is round, then Bob is blue (Rule 9).\n   - Big and quiet things are furry (Rule 10).\n   - If something is blue and furry, then it's smart (Rule 11).\n   - If Charlie is nice and big, then Charlie is quiet (Rule 12).\n\n2. **Logical Deduction**:\n   - From Rule 5, all nice things are big.\n   - Dave is nice (given fact).\n   - Therefore, Dave must be big.\n\nThus, the statement \"Dave is big\" is true.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
