ings are nice (Fact 10).\n   - Nice things are big (Fact 9).\n   - Kind, big things are young (Fact 13).\n   - If something is young and furry, it is quiet (Fact 11).\n   - If something is kind and not furry, it is not young (Fact 14).\n\n2. **Assume Harry is Kind:**\n   - If Harry is kind, he must be furry (Fact 8).\n   - Being furry makes him nice (Fact 10).\n   - Being nice makes him big (Fact 9).\n   - Being kind and big makes him young (Fact 13).\n   - Being young and furry makes him quiet (Fact 11).\n\n3. **Contradiction with Harry's Quietness:**\n   - The context does not explicitly state Harry's quietness, but from the given facts, if Harry were kind, he would have to be quiet. However, since we don't have information that Harry is quiet, we can't confirm this.\n\n4. **Alternative Approach:**\n   - If Harry is not quiet, then from Fact 11, he can't be both young and furry.\n   - If Harry is kind, he must be furry (Fact 8), which would require him to be quiet if he's young.\n   - Since we don't know Harry's quietness, we can't definitively say he's kind.\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   - There's insufficient information to conclusively determine whether Harry is kind or not. Therefore, the statement \"Harry is not kind\" is unknown.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
