e cat.So, the cat is seen by both the lion and the mouse, but we don\'t know the cat\'s feelings towards others.\n\nBut perhaps we can find out if the cat likes the lion or not.Let\'s see.\n\nFrom statement 11, if someone likes B.E.and B.E.doesn\'t like the cat, then the cat doesn\'t like the lion.We know someone (mouse) likes B.E.So, if B.E.doesn\'t like the cat, then the cat doesn\'t like the lion.\n\nBut we don\'t know if B.E.likes the cat or not.So, unless we can determine that, we can\'t apply statement 11.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can find out if the cat likes the lion or not through other means.\n\nWait, from statement 14: If someone likes the lion, then the lion is green.We know the lion is green because the mouse likes the lion (statement 15), so the lion is green (statement 14).So, the lion is green.\n\nFrom statement 18: If someone is green, then they like the B.E.So, the lion is green, so the lion likes the B.E.\n\nBut from statement 2, the B.E.does not like the lion.So, the B.E.doesn\'t like the lion, but the lion likes the B.E.\n\nNow, going back to statement 11: If someone likes B.E.and B.E.doesn\'t like the cat, then the cat doesn\'t like the lion.\n\nWe know someone (mouse) likes B.E.So, if B.E.doesn\'t like the cat, then the cat doesn\'t like the lion.\n\nBut we don\'t know if B.E.likes the cat or not.So, unless we can determine that, we can\'t conclude about the cat\'s feelings towards the lion.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can find out if the cat likes the lion or not through other means.\n\nWait, from statement 16: If someone eats the lion, then the lion eats the mouse.We know the mouse eats the lion (statement 7), so the lion eats the mouse.So, lion eats mouse.\n\nFrom statement 12: If someone eats the mouse, then the mouse is kind.We know the lion eats the mouse, so the mouse is kind.\n\nFrom statement 15: If someone is kind, then they like the lion.So, the mouse likes the lion.\n\nSo, the mouse likes the lion, which makes the lion green (