      "text": " B) False.\n\nWait, no, I think I made a mistake here. Let me go back.\n\nWait, point 15 says smart things are quiet. Point 16 says if something is smart and not rough, then it is quiet. But we know Gary is rough, so if he were smart, point 16 wouldn't apply because he is rough. But we don't know if Gary is smart or not.\n\nWait, point 12 says all smart things are white. Since Gary is white, he could be smart, but it's not necessarily the case. So, Gary might be smart or not.\n\nBut from point 14: If something is quiet and not smart, then it is kind. We know Gary is quiet. If he's not smart, then he must be kind. But from point 7, Gary is nice, but the context doesn't say he's kind. So, perhaps he's not kind, which would mean he must be smart because if he were not smart, he would have to be kind, which he isn't. Therefore, Gary must be smart.\n\nIf Gary is smart, then from point 15, smart things are quiet, which aligns with our earlier conclusion. Also, from point 12, all smart things are white, which is consistent because Gary is white.\n\nSo, Gary is smart, quiet, rough, red, and nice. Now, does this affect whether he's kind? From point 13, rough and quiet things are not kind. Since Gary is both rough and quiet, he cannot be kind. That's consistent because the context doesn't say Gary is kind, only that he's nice.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Gary is quiet. Therefore, the statement \"Gary is not quiet\" is false. So, the answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Gary is not quiet\" is false because based on the given information, Gary is both red and white, which according to the rules, makes him quiet. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\nB) False\n\n**Answer:** B) False",
