e white. So, if Gary is nice, he is white.\n\n- From point 13: If something is round and rough, it's nice.\n\n- From point 14: If something is rough, it's young.\n\n- From point 10: All young things are round.\n\nSo, if Gary is rough, he's young (point 14), and since all young things are round (point 10), he's round. From point 13, if he's round and rough, he's nice. From point 11, nice things are white. So, if Gary is rough, he's nice and white.\n\nBut Gary is cold (point 6). From point 12, rough and nice things are cold. So, if Gary is cold, he must be rough and nice, which makes him white.\n\nTherefore, Gary is white, so the statement \"Gary is not white\" is false. So the correct answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. Is there any possibility that Gary is not rough? If Gary is not rough, then he's not cold because point 12 says rough and nice things are cold. But Gary is cold, so he must be rough and nice, which makes him white.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Gary is not white\" is false. So the answer is B) False.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{B}\n</think>\n\nBased on the given context, we need to determine if the statement \"Gary is not white\" is true, false, or unknown.\n\n1. **Gary's Attributes**: Gary is cold (point 6) and quiet (point 7).\n2. **Rough and White Things**: Rough, white things are quiet (point 8). Since Gary is quiet, he could be rough and white.\n3. **Rough and Nice Things**: Rough, nice things are cold (point 12). Since Gary is cold, he could be rough and nice.\n4. **Nice Things**: Nice things are white (point 11). If Gary is nice, he is white.\n5. **Rough Things**: If something is rough, it is young (point 14). All young things are round (point 10). If something is round and rough, it is nice (point 13).\n6. **Conclusion**: Since Gary is cold, he must be rough and nice. Being nice makes him white. Therefore, Gary is white.\n\nThus, the statement \"Gary is not white\" is false.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
