ikes the cow.\n13.If something visits the squirrel, then it likes the cat.\n14.If something visits the cat, then it likes the cat.\n15.If something likes the squirrel and it is big, then the squirrel likes the lion.\n16.If something likes the cow and it visits the cow, then the cow visits the cat.\n17.If something likes the cat, then it chases the squirrel.\n18.If something chases the lion and the lion is not cold, then it visits the cow.\n19.If something chases the lion, then the lion chases the cow.\n20.If something chases the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cat.\n21.If the cow is rough, then the cow chases the cat.\n\nNow, the question is about the squirrel chasing itself.So, does the squirrel chase the squirrel?The statement in question is whether this is true or not.\n\nLet me see if any of the given statements directly or indirectly talk about the squirrel chasing itself.I don\'t see any statement that explicitly mentions the squirrel chasing itself.So, maybe I need to infer it from other statements.\n\nLooking at statement 12: The squirrel likes the cow.So, the squirrel has a liking towards the cow.\n\nStatement 13: If something visits the squirrel, then it likes the cat.So, if X visits the squirrel, X likes the cat.\n\nStatement 14: If something visits the cat, then it likes the cat.So, if X visits the cat, X likes the cat.\n\nStatement 15: If something likes the squirrel and it is big, then the squirrel likes the lion.So, if X likes the squirrel and X is big, then the squirrel likes the lion.\n\nStatement 16: If something likes the cow and it visits the cow, then the cow visits the cat.So, if X likes the cow and X visits the cow, then the cow visits the cat.\n\nStatement 17: If something likes the cat, then it chases the squirrel.So, if X likes the cat, X chases the squirrel.\n\nStatement 18: If something chases the lion and the lion is not cold, then it visits the cow.So, if X chases the lion and the lion is not cold, then X visits the cow.\n\nStatement 19: If something chases the lion, then the lion chases the cow.So, if X chases the lion, then the lion chases the cow.\n\nStatement 20: If something chases the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cat.So, if X chases the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cat.\n\nStatement 21: If the cow is rough, then the cow chases the cat.The cow is blue (statement 5), but I don\'t know if it\'s rough.The lion is rough (statement 10), but the cow\'s roughness isn\'t directly stated.However, statement 21 says if the cow is rough, then it chases the cat.\n\nNow, let\'s see if any of these can lead us to the squirrel chasing itself.\n\nFirst, from statement 12, the squirrel likes the cow.So, the squirrel has a liking towards the cow.\n\nLooking at statement 16: If something likes the cow and it visits the cow, then the cow visits the cat.So, if the squirrel likes the cow (which it does, from statement 12) and if the squirrel visits the cow, then the cow visits the cat.\n\nBut does the squirrel visit the cow?I don\'t have any statement that says the squirrel visits the cow.So, unless we can infer that, we can\'t proceed.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s look at statement 17: If something likes the cat, then it chases the squirrel.So, if X likes the cat, X chases the squirrel.\n\nDoes the squirrel like the cat?Let\'s see.The squirrel likes the cow (statement 12), but does it like the cat?I don\'t see any statement that says the squirrel likes the cat.So, unless we can infer that, the squirrel doesn\'t necessarily like the cat.\n\nWait, but statement 14 says if something visits the cat, then it likes the cat.So, if the squirrel visits the cat, then the squirrel likes the cat.But does the squirrel visit the cat?Let\'s see.\n\nLooking at statement 20: If something chases the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cat.So, if X chases the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cat.\n\nBut does anyone chase the squirrel?Let\'s see.Statement 17 says if X likes the cat, then X chases the s