      "text": " B) False.\n\nWait, no, that's not right. If the squirrel is big, then according to statement 14, it needs the squirrel. But the squirrel is itself, so it needs itself. But the statement in question is \"The squirrel does not need the squirrel.\" So if the squirrel needs itself, then the statement is false.\n\nWait, but the squirrel is big because it visits the bald eagle (statement 13). So, since the squirrel is big, statement 14 applies: if something is big, then it needs the squirrel. So the squirrel needs the squirrel. Therefore, the statement \"The squirrel does not need the squirrel\" is false.\n\nBut wait, the squirrel is the same as the squirrel, so needing itself is a bit of a paradox, but according to the rules, if it's big, it needs the squirrel, which is itself. So the statement is false.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. The squirrel is big because it visits the bald eagle (statement 10 and 13). So, being big, it needs the squirrel (statement 14). Therefore, the squirrel needs the squirrel, making the statement \"The squirrel does not need the squirrel\" false.\n\nSo the answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but I'm a bit confused because the squirrel is the same as the squirrel. So, does it make sense for the squirrel to need itself? In real life, no, but in this logical context, the rules apply regardless of real-world logic. So, according to the given statements, the squirrel is big, so it needs the squirrel, which is itself. Therefore, the statement that the squirrel does not need itself is false.\n\nYes, that makes sense. So the answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe squirrel is big because it visits the bald eagle (statement 13). As a big entity, it needs the squirrel (statement 14). Since the squirrel is itself, it needs itself. Therefore, the statement \"The squirrel does not need the squirrel\" is false.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
